{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a non-inductive metric, but this concept fails to solve the problem because, without inductive assumptions, there is no Popperian basis to believe that a theory with higher verisimilitude is more likely to succeed in future tests than one with lower verisimilitude. By advising scientists to 'prefer' the better-corroborated theory for action, the target implicitly endorses the principle that past performance (surviving tests) is a reliable guide to future utility, which smuggles back in the very inductive inference Popper rejected. The target claims this is merely a 'rational wager,' but a wager only makes sense if the odds are perceived to be favorable; asserting that the unrefuted theory is a 'viable candidate' while ignoring equally unrefuted alternatives with different future predictions requires an inductive leap that the target's mechanism does not successfully block.","premise":"Popper explicitly rejected the idea that corroboration implies probability or that the future will resemble the past.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be measured independently of inductive assumptions about the uniformity of nature, can any preference for a specific unfalsified theory over another truly be called 'non-inductive'?","target":"5e9de594afba8ef14af435e0a77e52ba402ff559e198a1b582ee283761e8e8b8"}]}